    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Find an antiderivative",
        "body": "Since $1-\\cos x=2\\sin^2\\frac x2$, $\\frac1{1-\\cos x}=\\frac1{2\\sin^2(x/2)}$ has antiderivative $-\\cot\\frac x2$, and $-\\frac2{x^2}$ has antiderivative $\\frac2x$. So $F(x)=-\\cot\\frac x2+\\frac2x$.\n\nAt $x=\\frac\\pi2$: $-1+\\frac4\\pi$. As $x\\to0^+$: $\\cot\\frac x2=\\frac2x-\\frac x6+\\cdots$, so $F(x)\\to0$. The integral is $\\frac4\\pi-1$.\n\n**Answer: $\\frac4\\pi-1$**"
      }
    ],
