    "prompt": "Evaluate $$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}n^2\\int_0^1x^ne^{-x}\\log(x)\\,dx.$$ (Here $\\log$ is the natural logarithm and $\\log^2x=(\\log x)^2$.)",
    "image": null,
    "choices": null,
    "answer": "-1/e",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Laplace's method",
        "body": "Substitute $x=e^{-y}$: $\\int_0^1x^ne^{-x}\\log x\\,dx=-\\int_0^\\infty y\\,e^{-(n+1)y}e^{-e^{-y}}dy$. For large $n$ only $y$ near $0$ matters, where $e^{-e^{-y}}\\to e^{-1}$, so the integral is $\\sim-e^{-1}\\int_0^\\infty ye^{-(n+1)y}dy=-\\frac{e^{-1}}{(n+1)^2}$. Multiplying by $n^2$ and letting $n\\to\\infty$ gives $-\\frac1e$.\n\n**Answer: $-\\frac1e$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Numerical check",
        "body": "Computing $n^2\\int_0^1x^ne^{-x}\\log x\\,dx$ numerically for $n=50,200,800$ gives $-0.3680$, $-0.3684$, $-0.3682$, approaching $-1/e=-0.3679$."
