    "prompt": "Evaluate $$\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lfloor x\\rfloor(1+\\{x\\})}\\,dx,$$ where $\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ is the greatest integer at most $x$ and $\\{x\\}=x-\\lfloor x\\rfloor$.",
    "image": null,
    "choices": null,
    "answer": "ln(1+e)",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Sum over unit intervals",
        "body": "On $[k,k+1)$ we have $\\lfloor x\\rfloor=k$, $\\{x\\}=t\\in[0,1)$. So the integral is $\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\int_0^1e^{-k(1+t)}dt$. The term $k=0$ is $1$. For $k\\ge1$: $\\int_0^1e^{-k-kt}dt=\\frac{e^{-k}(1-e^{-k})}{k}=\\frac{e^{-k}-e^{-2k}}k$.\n\nUsing $\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\frac{z^k}k=-\\log(1-z)$: $$\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\frac{e^{-k}-e^{-2k}}k=-\\log(1-e^{-1})+\\log(1-e^{-2})=\\log(1+e^{-1}).$$ Total: $1+\\log(1+e^{-1})=\\log e+\\log\\frac{e+1}e=\\log(1+e)$.\n\n**Answer: $\\log(1+e)$**"
      }
    ],
