    "answer": "pi*(2-sqrt(2))",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Reduce to a known integral",
        "body": "Define $J(c)=\\int_0^\\infty\\left(1-e^{-c/x^2}\\right)dx$. Substituting $x=\\sqrt c/s$ gives $J(c)=\\sqrt c\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{1-e^{-s^2}}{s^2}ds$, and integrating by parts, $\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{1-e^{-s^2}}{s^2}ds=\\left[-\\frac{1-e^{-s^2}}s\\right]_0^\\infty+\\int_0^\\infty2e^{-s^2}ds=\\sqrt\\pi$. So $J(c)=\\sqrt{\\pi c}$.\n\nNow $(1-a)^2=2(1-a)-(1-a^2)$ with $a=e^{-\\pi/x^2}$, and $a^2=e^{-2\\pi/x^2}$. Hence the integral is $2J(\\pi)-J(2\\pi)=2\\pi-\\pi\\sqrt2=\\pi(2-\\sqrt2)$.\n\n**Answer: $\\pi\\left(2-\\sqrt2\\right)$**"
      }
    ],
